title: "The Wednesday Route"
date: "2026-08-02"
excerpt: "On a warm Wednesday morning in the first week of August, at six-forty-two, a seventy-four-year-old retired mechanical engineer named Robert Everett Halvorsen — Bob, to everyone who has known him since the West Corning Junior High football team of 1963 — pulls his dark red 2011 Ford F-150 into the small crushed-limestone lot behind the Whistle Stop Bakery on Davis Street in Corning, Iowa, and orders — as he has ordered on the first Wednesday of every month since the first Wednesday of September, 2018 — forty small loaves of banana bread, wrapped in wax paper and twine, priced at six dollars a loaf, paid in cash from a plain brown billfold his wife Ruth Ann gave him for their fortieth anniversary in the June of 2013. Somewhere on the third floor of the Adams County Courthouse on the corner of Sixth and Davis, on the same Wednesday morning at eight-forty-nine, the record for Robert E. Halvorsen in the sustaining-donor file of Adams County Aging & Nutrition Services reads *$40/mo since 09/2018, ACH, mail only, no engagement noted, do not upgrade*. Behind the flour bin at the bakery, in a small navy-blue quadrille notebook the shop's owner Marlene Boedecker has kept on the second shelf above the walk-in cooler since a Wednesday in the September of 2018, is the small careful record of eight Septembers the courthouse file will not learn about until the November Bob finally, at the small oak desk in the front room, sits down to write it."
tag: "Field Notes"
---

# The Wednesday Route

Bob Halvorsen pulled the F-150 into the crushed-limestone lot behind the Whistle Stop at six-forty-two, set the parking brake with the same slow two-count he has used on every truck he has owned since a green 1976 Chevrolet Custom Deluxe in the spring of his second year at Southwest Iowa Rural Electric, and lifted the small brown billfold Ruth Ann gave him for their fortieth off the passenger seat.

Robert Everett "Bob" Halvorsen — Corning Community High class of 1969, Iowa State '73 (mechanical engineering, with a minor in agricultural systems under a professor named Dean Merle Pfleger whose small handwritten note about *the quiet decency of a load-bearing man* Bob still keeps folded inside the front of his 1972 Marks' Standard Handbook on the second shelf of the built-in in the front room), staff engineer at Southwest Iowa Rural Electric Cooperative on Highway 34 from the September of 1973 until his retirement on the last Friday of May, 2016 — has lived at 412 North 8th Street in Corning, Iowa, in a small clapboard bungalow with a white porch swing and a slate front step his son Peter re-set the summer his own boy Ethan turned four, since a warm Saturday afternoon in the September of 1976, when he and his wife Ruth Ann Halvorsen — Simpson College '73 (elementary education), first-grade teacher at Corning Elementary from the September of 1973 until her retirement in the June of 2014 — signed the deed at the Adams County Recorder's Office two blocks north.

Ruth Ann passed on the fourteenth of June, 2018, at four-eleven in the afternoon, in the small south-facing room on the second floor of Adams County Health System on Rosary Drive, of the pancreatic cancer Dr. Angela Bergman-Vermeer — a warm and unflinching woman Bob still, on Thanksgiving, mails a card to — had told them, in the January, was not going to see the corn tassel out.

The service was on the following Monday at Emmanuel Lutheran on 8th Street. Pastor Douglas Kroeger officiated. The luncheon was in the parish hall, on the folding tables the Ladies' Aid has been setting up in that hall since the November of 1959.

On the first Wednesday of September, three months later, at six-forty-two in the morning, Bob Halvorsen pulled the F-150 into the crushed-limestone lot behind the Whistle Stop Bakery on Davis Street, walked in through the back screen door, and ordered forty small loaves of banana bread.

He has done it every first Wednesday since.

## What the notebook above the walk-in cooler knows

Marlene Boedecker has been the owner of the Whistle Stop Bakery since the April of 2003.

She is sixty-three years old. She wears the same flour-dusted denim apron she has worn since her mother Adeline handed her the keys on a Tuesday morning at five-forty-five and drove down to a small block-brick house in Bella Vista, Arkansas, with her husband Elmer that same weekend. She keeps the shop's books in QuickBooks, on a small Dell laptop under the register. She keeps the *other* books — the ones that matter — in a small navy-blue quadrille notebook on the second shelf above the walk-in cooler. Not because she needs to, but because her mother did, in the same kind of notebook, from the November of 1979 until the January of 2003, and because a small handwritten line in a plain notebook is the only place certain things about a small town can be honestly kept.

On the fourth page of the notebook, in the section she has labeled, in her own square hand, *standing*, are nine names.

Nine regular customers who order the same small thing, on the same small schedule, every month — the widower on West Adams who orders a single small cardamom coffee cake for the first Sunday of Advent every year and has since 2011; the fourth-grade teacher out at Corning Elementary who orders a dozen small oatmeal-raisin cookies every second Friday for the little boy in her class whose mother left in the March of 2022; the sacristan of Emmanuel Lutheran, who calls on the Wednesday before Palm Sunday and orders the same three hundred and forty small dinner rolls for the Maundy Thursday supper the Ladies' Aid has served in the parish hall since the April of 1962.

The first name on the list is Bob Halvorsen.

*Halvorsen, R. — 1st Wed of month, 6:40 a.m. — 40 sm. loaves banana bread, wax paper + twine, $240 cash — for the Wednesday route.*

The line has been on that page since the fifth of September, 2018.

Underneath it, in the small careful hand Marlene uses when she does not want the customer to see her writing it down, are ninety-five smaller lines — one for each first Wednesday, from the September of 2018 until the July of 2026 — with the same small parenthetical addendum after eleven of them: *(also paid ahead for the Rodriguez apartment — Sylvia — $780 cash, plain envelope, no note)*.

Sylvia Rodriguez is thirty-nine years old. She is the kitchen coordinator for Adams County Meals on Wheels. She moved down to Corning from a small town outside Council Bluffs in the March of 2021, to a small second-floor walk-up above the barber shop on North 6th Street, and has, from that same March, paid her landlady Marjorie Kubale zero dollars a month in rent — because on the last Tuesday of every month, since the last Tuesday of February, 2021, a plain white envelope has arrived at Marjorie's mailbox at 314 East Benton with seven hundred and eighty dollars in cash inside, and nothing written on the outside except *Apt. 2 — Sylvia — August.*

Marjorie has never mentioned it to Sylvia.

Sylvia has never mentioned it to anyone at the county.

Nobody on the third floor of the Adams County Courthouse, on the corner of Sixth and Davis, has ever seen the notebook above the walk-in cooler. Nobody on the third floor has ever seen the plain white envelope in Marjorie's mailbox.

## What the third-floor file says

On the third floor of the Adams County Courthouse on the corner of Sixth and Davis, on the same Wednesday morning at eight-forty-nine, the new director of individual giving at Adams County Aging & Nutrition Services — a bright and diligent thirty-two-year-old named Jenna Coelho, hired in the February from the grants office at the Iowa Community Foundation in Des Moines — was opening the third-quarter sustaining-donor file on her laptop at a plain oak desk under the east window that faces the water tower.

The line for Robert Halvorsen came up on the forty-first row down.

*HALVORSEN, ROBERT E. — 412 N. 8th St., Corning IA 50841. Sustaining $40/mo since 09/2018. Initial memorial gift $500 (Ruth Ann Halvorsen, dec. 06/2018). ACH. Mail only. Spouse deceased. No engagement noted. Wealth screen: middle band. Do not upgrade.*

Jenna has never met Bob.

Jenna has never stood on the small covered stoop of the Whistle Stop Bakery on Davis Street on a first Wednesday morning at six-forty-two, and watched a seventy-four-year-old man in a Southwest Iowa REC cap lift a flat cardboard tray of forty small banana-bread loaves out of a dark red F-150 and set it, without a word, on the wide green stainless counter beside the register.

Jenna has never ridden Route 4 on a Wednesday afternoon at three-fifty-one, and watched Bob unwrap the wax paper and hand the small twine-tied loaf across the screen door of the small yellow farmhouse on 190th Street to a ninety-one-year-old great-aunt named Marilyn Halvorsen Kroeger, who does not, since the small stroke of the November of 2023, remember his name — but who takes the loaf, on the same small chipped white plate she has taken every other loaf on since the September of 2018, and holds it for a long moment against her cardigan, and says, in the same soft voice, *goodness, this is fresh, thank you, sir.*

The wealth screen has never opened the envelope in the mailbox at 314 East Benton.

The wealth screen does not know about the October of 2022, or about Bob's grandfather Everett's 320-acre corn ground on the west side of Adams County that Bob and his sister Diane and his brother Karl sold, on a Wednesday morning at ten past ten in a small conference room at the Adams County State Bank on Davis Street, to a young farming family from outside Villisca for two million four hundred thousand dollars. It does not know that Bob's share is at a Vanguard money-market account in Malvern, Pennsylvania, at three-point-one percent, and that Bob, in the eleven quarters since, has not touched it.

The wealth screen does not know that Bob is quietly, in the plainest possible envelope, on the last Tuesday of every month, paying his own kitchen coordinator's rent.

## What twenty years of software has quietly missed

For twenty years, every honest county aging office in America has been buying — one annual contract at a time, at prices that would keep Sylvia Rodriguez in a walk-up above the barber shop on 6th Street for the next thirteen years — tools that were built to answer the wrong question about Bob Halvorsen.

The tools were built to answer: *of our four thousand two hundred donor records, which ones look most like the ones who wrote the largest checks last year?*

Bob has not written a large check.

Bob has been writing a different kind of check — quietly, in twenties and fifties, from the small brown billfold Ruth Ann gave him — every first Wednesday morning at the back door of a bakery on Davis Street, and every last Tuesday evening at the blue mailbox on the corner of 6th and Benton — for eight Septembers.

That is a different kind of check. It requires a different instrument.

The instrument that could see Bob is not a wealth screen. It is a small careful set of notes, assembled by someone who has spent a quiet Tuesday morning listening — to the *Adams County Free Press* retirement notice for R. E. Halvorsen from the June of 2016, to the *Ruth Ann Halvorsen* obituary from the sixteenth of June, 2018, to the small line in the Emmanuel Lutheran Sunday bulletin every first Sunday since October, 2018, that reads *coffee hour donuts this week given in loving memory of Ruth Ann, by Bob*, to the tiny three-line item the *Free Press* ran on the twelfth of October, 2022, when a young Villisca farming family bought a 320-acre parcel from the Halvorsen heirs for a sum the paper printed without commas — and set them beside Bob's file, and hand them to Jenna on a Monday morning at eight-fifty, before the third-quarter portfolio meeting.

The notes would say:

*Halvorsen, Robert E. Retired staff engineer, Southwest Iowa REC, 1973–2016. Widower of Ruth Ann Halvorsen, first-grade teacher at Corning Elementary 1973–2014, deceased June 2018 in the oncology unit at Adams County Health System under Dr. Angela Bergman-Vermeer. Volunteers Wednesday afternoons on Meals on Wheels Route 4; last stop is his great-aunt Marilyn Halvorsen Kroeger, 91, at 2214 190th St. On the first Wednesday of every month since September 2018, Bob has purchased and paid for, in cash, forty small loaves of banana bread from Whistle Stop Bakery — one loaf for every homebound client the four Meals on Wheels routes serve that day — a gift the county has not, in eight years, recorded. In October 2022, Bob and his siblings sold Everett Halvorsen's 320-acre ground west of Corning for $2.4M; his one-third share sits in a Vanguard money-market account he has not touched. Since March 2021, Bob has additionally paid, in unmarked cash, the $780/mo rent on kitchen coordinator Sylvia Rodriguez's second-floor walk-up above the barber shop at 314 East Benton. Suggestion, this September: no ask. A handwritten letter from Sylvia Rodriguez, herself, thanking Bob — by name — for the ninety-six first Wednesdays of banana bread, and mentioning that the small chipped white plate Marilyn Kroeger takes the loaf on every Wednesday afternoon is the same plate Bob's mother Della gave Marilyn for her wedding in the June of 1954. Mail it from Sylvia's own return address on 6th Street, not the courthouse. Bob will fold it into the front of the 1972 Marks' Standard Handbook on the second shelf of the built-in in the front room, beside the small note from Dean Pfleger about the quiet decency of a load-bearing man.*

Jenna, on a Monday morning at eight-fifty, reads the notes. She looks at them for a long moment. She walks down to 314 East Benton that afternoon.

That is the whole product.

## We are not the Wednesday afternoon

Rōmy is not, and cannot be, the drive from Corning to 190th Street at three-fifty-one on a Wednesday afternoon in the first week of August.

The dark red F-150 is Bob's. The wax paper and twine are Marlene's. The small brown billfold Ruth Ann gave him for their fortieth is Bob's. The small chipped white plate Marilyn Kroeger takes the loaf on every Wednesday is Della Halvorsen's, from the June of 1954. The blue mailbox on the corner of 6th and Benton is the county's. The small unmarked envelope with the seven hundred and eighty dollars inside — *Apt. 2 — Sylvia — August* — is Bob's, and it will stay Bob's, and it is the whole gift, and the whole reason there will be a forty-first loaf on the counter at the Whistle Stop on the first Wednesday of September.

What a tool can do — quietly, on the third floor of a courthouse on the corner of Sixth and Davis on a Monday morning at eight-fifty, twenty minutes before Jenna opens the CRM — is hand her the small careful page a good listener would have written if a good listener had been sitting on the counter stool by the register at the Whistle Stop on Davis Street, watching the first-Wednesday loaves come in through the back screen door.

That is a smaller job than the industry has been trying to sell for twenty years.

It is also, we think, with some love, the whole job.

## A small assignment, with love

Walk into the small bakery in your service area this week. Not the chain one on the highway, with the drive-through window and the mobile app. The other one. The one with the flour-dusted apron, the walk-in cooler with the loose gasket at the top left corner, and the small handwritten card taped to the register that reads *no orders over the phone after 2 — please stop in.*

Ask the woman behind the counter, when the shop is quiet — after the Wednesday-morning wholesale pickup and before the Thursday-noon lunch rush — how many standing orders she has.

Ask her, if she will tell you, which are the widowers.

Then, on Monday morning, before you open the CRM — before the mail-merge window suggests a forty-dollar-a-month sustainer on the forty-first row down — pull down a plain notecard from the top drawer, and write, in your own hand, the two sentences a first-Wednesday-of-the-month baker would have written for you, if she were paid, in this life, to write them.

Sign it.

Mail it from your kitchen table on Saturday morning, so the postmark reads the small town on the county road where your donor's great-aunt still lives.

The F-150 is still in the drive at 412 North 8th.

The forty small loaves are still on the counter at the Whistle Stop.

Marlene has been keeping the notebook above the walk-in cooler since the April of 2003. ♡
